{"name":"Totem Pole","hex":"#991b07","fetched":"2026-10-13","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"totem pole","whole":true,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A Native American sculpture found in the Pacific Northwest, made by carving trees, and usually displaying symbolic figures that represent clan lineage, cultural beliefs, or historical events.","A hierarchy or ranking of importance."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/totem_pole"}],"entries":[{"title":"Totem pole","blocks":[{"text":"Totem poles are monumental carvings found in western Canada and the northwestern United States. They are a type of Indigenous Northwest Coast art, consisting of poles, posts or pillars, carved with symbols or figures. They are usually made from large trees, mostly western red cedar, by First Nations and Indigenous peoples of the Pacific Northwest Coast including northern Northwest Coast Haida, Tlingit, and Tsimshian communities in Southeast Alaska and British Columbia, Kwakwaka'wakw and Nuu-chah-nulth communities in southern British Columbia, and the Coast Salish communities in Washington and British Columbia."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/68/Totem_RMBC_1.jpg/330px-Totem_RMBC_1.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Totem_pole"},{"title":"Totem Pole (Tasmania)","blocks":[{"text":"The Totem Pole is a sea stack popular amongst rockclimbers in the Tasman National Park, Tasmania, Australia. It contains a number of climbing routes, all of which require a Tyrolean traverse to return to the mainland, and is famous for being the site of the 1998 accident which caused British climber Paul Pritchard's hemiplegia."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/28/The_Totem_Pole_and_The_Candlestick_%2835113792552%29.jpg/330px-The_Totem_Pole_and_The_Candlestick_%2835113792552%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Totem_Pole_(Tasmania)"},{"title":"Totem","blocks":[{"text":"A totem is a spirit being, sacred object, or symbol that serves as an emblem of a group of people, such as a family, clan, lineage, or tribe, such as in the Anishinaabe clan system."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/45/Kwakiutl1.jpg/330px-Kwakiutl1.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Totem"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/df/%27Ksan_Historical_Village_2010.jpg/500px-%27Ksan_Historical_Village_2010.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/df/%27Ksan_Historical_Village_2010.jpg/1280px-%27Ksan_Historical_Village_2010.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/df/%27Ksan_Historical_Village_2010.jpg/500px-%27Ksan_Historical_Village_2010.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Totem poles and houses at ʼKsan, near Hazelton, British Columbia.","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3A'Ksan_Historical_Village_2010.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/97/Houses_And_Totem_Poles_Of_Alaskan_Indians_%E2%80%94_Official_Views_Of_The_World%27s_Columbian_Exposition_%E2%80%94_89.jpg/500px-Houses_And_Totem_Poles_Of_Alaskan_Indians_%E2%80%94_Official_Views_Of_The_World%27s_Columbian_Exposition_%E2%80%94_89.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/97/Houses_And_Totem_Poles_Of_Alaskan_Indians_%E2%80%94_Official_Views_Of_The_World%27s_Columbian_Exposition_%E2%80%94_89.jpg/1280px-Houses_And_Totem_Poles_Of_Alaskan_Indians_%E2%80%94_Official_Views_Of_The_World%27s_Columbian_Exposition_%E2%80%94_89.jpg?utm_sourc